A||N||3||Carenath||2.5L  Tapping in head - Rocker arm?||||1||
Z||000000||Carenath||10-28-2001||01:48 AM||||Heyas guys, been researching your posts here and looking for advice.  I've got a 2.5L Fiero that I've been working on restoring and have stumbled into some interesting puzzles as of late tracking down the last of the significant problems.  First of all, thanks to this archive I was able to correct for the problem with my EGR vacumm leak. Turns out I just needed to use some RTV Blue sealant on it to create a enhancement to the gasket and then tighten it down firmly and that did the job.  My idle is back where its supposed to be, well, sortof.. but good enough for the time being (1000-1200).  The reason for my post here is in regards to my current puzzle.  Within the last couple months it seems my engine has a odd tapping sound that I'm not certain if its a vibration or not.  When I start the engine it will idle high (~2000rpms) until it reaches what appears to be 110 on the temp guage, at which time it will drop down to ~1000-1200 rpms.  During the time in which it is idleing high however, and during accelleration, there is a tapping that comes from the left side of the engine (as seen standing at the rear of the car looking at the engine; the side opposite the crank pully).  I'm not certain exactly what this means but I've been told it could be a loose rocker arm.  If so, while a bit apprehensive about going inside my head I feel I could handle tightening this down properly.  I however would like to know if there are any other ideas as to what this would be.  The tapping is noticable at the low idle of about 1000 but becomes noticable as you increase the rpms.  Also I will note that my oil pressure generally holds around 60lbs which seems odd.  Any suggestions as to what this could be and how to correct it would be greatly appreciated.||||reg||1
Z||000001||MrPBody||10-28-2001||08:37 AM||||Welcome to the forum!<P>There is no variable valve / rocker arm adjustment on a 2.5.  If your noise comes from the valve train, then something is loose, worn, or broken.<P>If a rocker arm bolt is loose, tighten it to the torque specified in your service manual.  (I don't remember.)<P>Working under the valve cover isn't too tough, just a little messy.  Replace the valve cover gasket while you're in there, of course.<P>What year is your car?  I have read some past posts regarding valve train problems in '87 - '88 Dukes.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||10-28-2001||12:17 PM||||Sounds like there's still a vac leak someplace. read the notes in my cave fro more things to chack.<P>Use a mechanical gauge to verify the dash gauge is working corrrectly. sounds like normal pressure to me.<P>The 87+ motors are inherantly noisy....<P>That said...<P>I hope you used "sensor safe" sealer or you may find the O2 sensor ruined in the near future.<P>You'll get a new EGR gasket with the new valve cover gasket. The EGR has to be removed to get the valve cover off.<P>Get a rubber gasket and install it dry. The I think permatex high tack gasket adhesive is allowed but that's about it. The rubber gaskets seal much better than cork and properly installed are unlikely to fail any time soon.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">    <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 10-28-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Carenath||10-28-2001||02:10 PM||||First off, I have an 86 Fiero.  I am going to be changing the sealant soon on the EGR anyways as I had used the RTV Blue on it which is not going to be happy w/ the O2 Sensor.  It was basicly a quick fix so I could determine if that were the problem for sure.  I've reinforced the seal on all of the vacumm lines I could get to and at this point have determined that my main enemy is the EGR Valve as tightening it down like I have has eliminated all but a trace of the problems i was having before at least on the fuel/air mix and sensor malfunction spectrum.||||reg||1
